    context: Television made its dramatic public debut with the 1936 Olympic Games broadcast in Berlin, foreshadowing its power to create shared mass experiences. Television's transformation of political communication in America followed a pattern similar to radio but with even more dramatic consequences. Television ownership exploded from 9% of households in 1950 to 65% by 1960 [127]. The federal government again supported infrastructure development, though less directly than with radio. Crucially, early television maintained the same mono-channel culture as radio---with only three major networks (ABC, CBS, NBC) dominating the airwaves, ensuring that most Americans received the same visual political diet. This shared viewing experience created a powerful common frame of reference for political events.
