      The reference's focus on television's invention is particularly relevant to understanding how this technology evolved from experimental beginnings to become the infrastructure supporting the mono-channel culture described in the manuscript. By documenting television's historical development through Census Bureau records, this source provides governmental perspective on a technology that would fundamentally transform American political communication. This official recognition of television's significance underscores the medium's importance to American society and helps explain why federal support for television infrastructure development, though less direct than for radio, was still considered important. The reference thus supports the manuscript's broader narrative about how television created shared mass experiences and common frames of reference for political events in mid-twentieth-century America.
