    citation: '[102] Kraus, S. (2000). Televised Presidential Debates and Public Policy. (2nd ed) Routledge, New York/London, ISBN: 9780805816037.'
    finalized: true
    context: The visual contrast was so pronounced that it fundamentally altered American political communication. Kraus's comprehensive analysis [102] of this watershed moment reveals how radio listeners thought Nixon won while television viewers favored Kennedy---demonstrating how visual media privileges image over argument. This finding perfectly embodied Marshall McLuhan's emerging insight that "the medium is the message"---the television medium itself, not just the content transmitted through it, fundamentally shaped how audiences perceived and evaluated the candidates. The debate established that television success required not just rhetorical skill but telegenic appeal, comfort with the medium, and careful attention to visual presentation. This single debate demonstrated that in the television age, how a candidate looked would matter as much as, if not more than, what they said.
    relevance: Kraus's comprehensive study examines the history and impact of televised presidential debates on American political discourse, with particular attention to the landmark 1960 Kennedy-Nixon debates. In the context of the manuscript's discussion of television's transformation of political communication, this reference provides foundational empirical evidence for the claim that visual media fundamentally altered how candidates are evaluated. Specifically, Kraus documents the striking divergence between radio listeners' and television viewers' perceptions of debate winners, demonstrating that the medium itself—not merely the rhetorical content—shaped audience judgments. This finding serves as concrete evidence for the manuscript's argument that television privileges visual presentation over substantive argument, marking a paradigm shift in political communication. The reference supports the broader thesis about television's role in elevating "image over argument" by providing systematic analysis of how the visual medium changed the criteria for political success. Kraus's work validates the manuscript's claim that the 1960 debate was a watershed moment, establishing new standards where telegenic appeal and visual presentation became as important as, or more important than, traditional oratorical skill. This scholarly analysis gives academic credibility to the manuscript's assertion that this single event exemplified McLuhan's "medium is the message" principle in practical political terms.
