    relevance: 'Hallin and Mancini''s seminal work establishes a comparative framework for analyzing media systems across democratic societies, identifying three distinct models: the Mediterranean Polarized Pluralist model, the Northern European Democratic Corporatist model, and the North Atlantic Liberal model. In the context of the manuscript''s discussion of American media transformation following the Fairness Doctrine''s repeal, this reference provides essential theoretical grounding for understanding how media deregulation fundamentally altered the U.S. media landscape. The authors'' Liberal model framework helps explain why the American broadcasting system was particularly susceptible to polarization after 1987, as market-driven media in liberal systems typically exhibit weaker political parallelism and greater commercial orientation than other models. This theoretical lens illuminates the manuscript''s narrative about how deregulation enabled partisan media—initially through talk radio, then Fox News—to emerge and thrive. The reference contextualizes the shift from the Cronkite-era consensus journalism to polarized media ecosystems within broader patterns of media system development. By situating the post-Fairness Doctrine transformation within comparative media systems theory, Hallin and Mancini''s framework helps explain why American media evolved toward fragmentation and partisanship rather than maintaining the institutional checks that characterize other democratic media systems, thereby supporting the manuscript''s analysis of how structural changes enabled ideological segmentation.'
