    finalized: true
    context: 'Our condition is total dependency on memory—both internal and external. Bernard Stiegler diagnosed the "industrialization of memory"—the process by which this natural dependency has been captured and controlled at an industrial scale [205]. Stiegler identifies three forms of memory: primary retention (the immediate flow of consciousness—what''s happening right now in your awareness), secondary retention (our internal, biological memory stored in the brain—what we remember and can recall from our own minds), and tertiary retention (all forms of externalized, technical memory—from cave paintings and writing to photographs, films, and digital databases).'
    relevance: |-
      Stiegler's *For a New Critique of Political Economy* develops a philosophical framework examining how technical systems and memory have become subject to capitalist industrialization, fundamentally altering human consciousness and social relations. The work establishes Stiegler's tripartite conceptualization of memory—primary retention (immediate conscious experience), secondary retention (biological memory), and tertiary retention (externalized technical memory)—which serves as the theoretical foundation for understanding how memory functions have been captured by industrial processes.

      In the context of the manuscript's Section 2.3 on the externalization of memory, this reference provides the essential conceptual apparatus for analyzing human dependency on external memory systems. Stiegler's framework directly supports the manuscript's argument by theorizing how the natural human reliance on memory has been transformed through industrial-scale capture and control. The reference supplies the critical terminology and analytical categories needed to distinguish between different memory forms and their relationships to technological and economic systems.

      Most importantly, Stiegler's concept of "industrialization of memory" offers the manuscript a precise theoretical lens for understanding contemporary conditions of memory externalization. This enables the analysis to move beyond descriptive accounts of technology use toward a critical examination of how memory systems operate within larger economic and political structures, making this reference foundational to the section's theoretical framework.
